> 1. Is Nethack written with good style?

NetHack code has accumulated over many years and is compatible with a
lot of environments. Reading it can be educational, but you'll need to
know how to separate the noise from the good stuff, i.e. it's not good
for learning C.

> I also read in K&R that the excessive use of macros is deprecated.
> Is that not true for larger programs?

NetHack is a sort of program where much of the code functions in the
same logical context. There are also many global values and constants
like object/monster types, states, "arbitrary values", etc. and
checks/operations with them. This inherently brings an argument for
heavy macro usage. If NetHack was written now, I suppose it would be
implemented quite differently (object orientedness seems quite a good
choice for roguelikes), but much of it seems to be written in the
80s/early 90s and the DevTeam hasn't been exactly progressive since.

> 2. In general, how do you go about finding methods that are being
> called?

At runtime: gdb (1). To find function/macro declarations with a few
keystrokes, you can use ctags (1) to create an index of keywords and
use an editor that supports it. If you need auto-completion and such
stuff, it might be best to have a look at the newer free IDEs (haven't
really used any of them).

